  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We propose a symmetry-based optimization approach that leverages reflection symmetry about x=1 (h(x) = h(2-x)) to reduce the dimensionality of the search space.\n   - This reduces the effective number of variables by half (from 160 to 80 in our implementation), significantly decreasing computational complexity while potentially discovering more structured solutions.\n   - The core idea is to parameterize the function by its values on [0,1] and use reflection symmetry to define it on [1,2], ensuring the domain is appropriately closed and overlaps are minimized at natural symmetry points.\n   - We expect this to produce better quality solutions than previous approaches since symmetry is known to play a crucial role in optimal function shapes for the minimum overlap problem.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Domain: [0,2] with reflection symmetry about x=1\n   - Variables: h:[0,2] \u2192 [0,1] satisfying h(x) = h(2-x) for all x\n   - Objective: min\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2\u207b\u1d4f h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   - Representation: Use a symmetric discrete representation with m points on [0,1] to generate n_points = 2m points\n   - Constraints:\n     - $\\sum h_i \\, \\Delta x = 1$\n     - $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$\n     - Symmetry: $h_{i} = h_{n_{\\text{points}}-1-i}$\n   - The reflection symmetry constraint effectively reduces the number of free variables by half.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - a. Parameterize using half-length representation: v = [v\u2080, v\u2081, ..., v_{m-1}] defines h on the first half [0,1]\n   - b. Automatically construct full-length function: h[i] = v[i//(n_points//2)] if i < n_points//2 else v[n_points//2-1-i//(n_points//2)]\n   - c. Compute objective: use np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') then take max * (2/len(h))\n   - d. Enforce constraints via:\n        - Box constraints (0-1)\n        - Integral constraint: compute (2/n_points) * \u2211h[j] == 1 with tolerance\n   - e. Optimization:\n        - Use SLSQP (SciPy's Sequential Least Squares Programming)\n        - Initialize with smoothed periodic structure (for symmetry) or parent if provided\n        - Enhance exploration with line search\n   - f. Restart when domain rotation (about multiples of \u03c0) occurs\n   - g. If parent available, create 8 fixed initializations for robustness\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Base resolution: n_points=16384 (extremely fine) \n   - Structure types: 3-parameter power envelope, cosine pack, and modified sinusoidal for symmetry\n   - Parameters: amplitude, width, offset for each structure class\n   - Optimization: 5000 steps with worst-case 350ms/step\n   - Population diversity: evolve through symmetry enforcements and parameter rotations\n   - Target noise fraction: formally ~1e-4, actually 2e-3 for better signal\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - n_points=16384 requires ~2.7e6 integral evaluations per optimization run\n   - Estimate: ~5000 \u00d7 350ms = 36 minutes total per strategy\n   - Monitor: deferred for speed (but ensures solution validates)\n   - Fallback: Return best known child solution from previous strategy\n   - Optimization order: Smooth envelope then periodic modulation then Gaussian convolution\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Input validation: stop if h_vals not in [0,1] or sum!\u22480.5/n_points tolerance\n   - Parallel checkpoints: enforce validity (sum close to 0.5/n) and objective min via surrogate formula\n   - Re-evaluation: final solution accepting only if meets Jensen lower bound and passes objective derivative check\n   - Return format: (h_normalized, C_value, n_points=confident_resolution)",
